I"�<h2 id="the-question">The question</h2>

<p>Parkinson’s and Alzheimer’s are the two most common neurodegenerative diseases and a huge global burden. Understanding them depends on knowledge that curators extract by hand from a vast literature. In the ApiNATOMY project, a curator would read each paper and mark the passages linking a test to brain anatomy. That is slow and costly.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-did">What we did</h2>

<p>We built NapEasy, a tool that automatically highlights the relevant sentences in a paper’s PDF. This is related to summarising a paper, but harder, because the number of sentences to highlight varies a lot from paper to paper.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-found">What we found</h2>

<p>On a corrected test set, the tool reached a macro-averaged F1 of 0.51, which we saw as a promising first step. In a user evaluation, the highlights significantly helped curation in most cases, and in about 65% of cases the highlights were enough to do the curation task without reading the full text.</p>

<h2 id="why-it-matters">Why it matters</h2>

<p>Pre-digesting papers for curators means faster processing and lower cost. The code is open on GitHub.</p>
